= Upgrade from v0.3.0

This page lists the changes needed to move a contract from OpenZeppelin Contracts for Stylus v0.3.0 to the SDK 0.10.10 release of this library. The library's stored fields and Solidity selectors are preserved, except for the explicitly described behavior hardening below. Existing proxy storage does not need a migration.

== Pin the supported toolchain

Use stable Rust 1.91.0 and Stylus SDK 0.10.10. Update direct Alloy dependencies to the 1.7.3 family, and allow `ruint` 1.17.1 or newer. The SDK uses `ruint` with its standard library defaults disabled in WASM builds.

[source,toml]
----
[dependencies]
stylus-sdk = "=0.10.10"
alloy-primitives = { version = "=1.7.3", default-features = false }
alloy-sol-types = { version = "=1.7.3", default-features = false }
ruint = "^1.17.1"
----

Replace nightly-only build commands such as `cargo +nightly build -Z build-std` with the stable toolchain's configured `wasm32-unknown-unknown` target.

== Pass VM context explicitly

SDK 0.10 uses VM accessors. Replace global calls such as `msg::sender()`, `msg::value()`, `block::timestamp()`, and `contract::address()` with methods on the contract's VM:

[source,rust]
----
let sender = self.vm().msg_sender();
let value = self.vm().msg_value();
let timestamp = self.vm().block_timestamp();
let address = self.vm().contract_address();
let has_code = self.vm().code_size(target) != 0;
----

Cross-contract interfaces now receive the VM and an explicit call context. Use `Call::new()` for a read-only call and `Call::new_mutating(self)` for a mutating call:

[source,rust]
----
let token = Erc20Interface::new(token_address);
let balance = token.balance_of(self.vm(), Call::new(), owner)?;

let call = Call::new_mutating(self);
token.transfer(self.vm(), call, recipient, amount)?;
----

Methods that access VM state must satisfy the SDK's `HostAccess` bounds. The public `StorageSlot::get_slot` helper also takes a VM-accessible context as its first argument: call `StorageSlot::get_slot(self, slot)` from a contract method. EIP-712 `chain_id` and `contract_address` hooks are instance methods in the new API and should read those values from `self.vm()`.

Move `MethodError` imports to `stylus_sdk::stylus_core::calls::errors::MethodError`.

== Revisit raw calls and reentrancy

SDK 0.10.5 deprecated the `reentrant` entrypoint guard because high-level calls now flush storage before transferring control. In SDK 0.10.10, the guard is still enabled by default. Enable the library's `reentrant` feature for contracts whose external callback must call back into the same contract; this forwards the SDK feature and disables the guard. The flash-mint example enables it by default because borrower callbacks commonly read or call the lender token during the loan. Consumers should enable it only when their contract needs reentrant calls and should still follow checks-effects-interactions.

[source,toml]
----
[dependencies]
openzeppelin-stylus = { version = "0.4", features = ["reentrant"] }
----

The feature does not make arbitrary reentrant logic safe. Review every external call and state invariant. Raw calls still require care: if a raw call can reenter and read state written earlier in the current call, flush and clear the VM cache before the call with `self.vm().flush_cache(true)`.

The library's SafeERC20 raw call path does this explicitly. Prefer typed SDK interface calls in new code when their return-value contract fits the token being called.

== Validate ABI return data

Use Alloy's validating decoders for untrusted ABI data. In particular, SafeERC20 accepts an empty return or a canonical ABI `true`, and rejects malformed boolean words. If your own code previously used a permissive decoder, switch to its `*_validate` form and handle decoding errors.

== Update tests

The old `motsu` unit-test framework and this repository's `e2e` / `e2e-proc` crates were removed. For storage-only unit tests, enable `stylus-sdk`'s `stylus-test` feature and construct a fresh `TestVM` per contract under test. Set sender, contract address, block values, storage, and mocked host calls on that VM. A second `TestVM` is an independent host; it does not automatically become a deployed external contract.

Run cross-contract, proxy, transaction rollback, activation, and gas behavior against the SDK devnet tools in `stylus-tools`. In this repository, run those tests with:

[source,shell]
----
./scripts/e2e-tests.sh
----

The SDK devnet is started and managed by the test harness through Docker. Remove `e2e` and `motsu` dependencies from downstream test manifests and use the SDK test framework or `stylus-tools::devnet::Node` directly.

== Public API and behavior notes

* ERC-20 cap checks can be shared through `Capped::check_increase(total_supply, amount)`. It returns the standard `ExceededCap` error payload and does not change storage.
* `Erc721Wrapper` now rejects deposits, withdrawals, and receiver callbacks when its configured underlying address has no code. Previously, an EOA could make a no-return call appear successful, allowing an unbacked wrapper mint or a burn with no underlying transfer, or could call the receiver callback directly to mint an unbacked token. This is an intentional security fix; configure a deployed ERC-721 contract before using the wrapper.
* `Erc20Wrapper::_recover` now reports the configured underlying token in `ERC20InvalidUnderlying` when the underlying `balanceOf` call fails. The previous error encoded the wrapper's own address.
* Contracts implementing `IErc721Receiver` must expose the canonical `onERC721Received` selector. The receiver trait now specifies it explicitly so generated ABI and interface ID metadata agree with ERC-721.
* Poseidon2 construction now rejects round exponents that are not invertible modulo `p - 1`. Such exponents do not define permutations and can produce collisions. Review any custom Poseidon2 parameter sets and select a valid exponent.
* Contract storage fields, proxy slots, event signatures, and public function selectors are unchanged by the SDK migration. The behavior changes above affect invalid configurations or invalid parameter sets only.

== Rust API migration details

* Replace `stylus_sdk::msg`, `block`, and `contract` global accessors with `self.vm()` methods. Functions that need VM state now take `&self` or `&mut self` and satisfy the SDK `HostAccess` bounds.
* Add the VM and a `Call` context to generated `sol_interface!` calls. Use `Call::new()` for read-only calls and `Call::new_mutating(self)` for state-changing calls.
* Import `MethodError` from `stylus_sdk::stylus_core::calls::errors`.
* Update `IEip712` implementations so `chain_id` and `contract_address` are instance methods, and pass an appropriate VM context to `StorageSlot::get_slot` and proxy utility functions that read VM state.
* Alloy 1.7 generated contract clients return decoded values directly from `.call().await?`; remove old `.field` accesses on single-value responses.
* Replace Motsu unit tests with SDK `TestVM`, and move integration tests from the removed `e2e` crates to SDK devnet tooling. `TestVM` instances are isolated host environments, not deployed external contracts.

== Rebuild and check

Build every example and template for WASM with stable Rust, run the SDK check and activation against a devnet, and compare exported ABI and storage layout before upgrading a live proxy. Keep the same `#[storage]` field order and types; new fields in a deployed storage struct require an explicit storage-layout review.
= Access Control

Access control—that is, "who is allowed to do this thing"—is incredibly important in the world of smart contracts. The access control of your contract may govern who can mint tokens, vote on proposals, freeze transfers, and many other things. It is therefore *critical* to understand how you implement it, lest someone else https://blog.openzeppelin.com/on-the-parity-wallet-multisig-hack-405a8c12e8f7[steals your whole system].

[[ownership-and-ownable]]
== Ownership and `Ownable`

The most common and basic form of access control is the concept of _ownership_: there's an account that is the `owner` of a contract and can do administrative tasks on it. This approach is perfectly reasonable for contracts that have a single administrative user.

OpenZeppelin Contracts for Stylus provides https://docs.rs/openzeppelin-stylus/0.4.0/openzeppelin_stylus/access/ownable/struct.Ownable.html[`Ownable`] for implementing ownership in your contracts.

[source,rust]
----
use openzeppelin_stylus::access::ownable::{self, Ownable};

#[derive(SolidityError, Debug)]
enum Error {
    UnauthorizedAccount(ownable::OwnableUnauthorizedAccount),
    InvalidOwner(ownable::OwnableInvalidOwner),
    // other custom errors...
}

impl From<ownable::Error> for Error {
    fn from(value: ownable::Error) -> Self {
        match value {
            ownable::Error::UnauthorizedAccount(e) => {
                Error::UnauthorizedAccount(e)
            }
            ownable::Error::InvalidOwner(e) => Error::InvalidOwner(e),
        }
    }
}

#[entrypoint]
#[storage]
struct OwnableExample {
    ownable: Ownable,
}

#[public]
#[implements(IOwnable<Error = Error>)]
impl MyContract {
    fn normal_thing(&self) {
        // anyone can call this normal_thing()
    }

    fn special_thing(&mut self) -> Result<(), Error> {
        self.ownable.only_owner()?;

        // only the owner can call special_thing()!

        Ok(())
    }
}

#[public]
impl IOwnable for MyContract {
    type Error = Error;

    fn owner(&self) -> Address {
        self.ownable.owner()
    }

    fn transfer_ownership(
        &mut self,
        new_owner: Address,
    ) -> Result<(), Self::Error> {
        Ok(self.ownable.transfer_ownership(new_owner)?)
    }

    fn renounce_ownership(&mut self) -> Result<(), Self::Error> {
        Ok(self.ownable.renounce_ownership()?)
    }
}
----

At deployment, the https://docs.rs/openzeppelin-stylus/0.4.0/openzeppelin_stylus/access/ownable/struct.Ownable.html#method.owner[`owner`] of an `Ownable` contract is set to the provided `initial_owner` parameter.

Ownable also lets you:

* https://docs.rs/openzeppelin-stylus/0.4.0/openzeppelin_stylus/access/ownable/struct.Ownable.html#method.transfer_ownership[`transfer_ownership`] from the owner account to a new one, and
* https://docs.rs/openzeppelin-stylus/0.4.0/openzeppelin_stylus/access/ownable/struct.Ownable.html#method.renounce_ownership[`renounce_ownership`] for the owner to relinquish this administrative privilege, a common pattern after an initial stage with centralized administration is over.

WARNING: Removing the owner altogether will mean that administrative tasks that are protected by `only_owner` will no longer be callable!

Note that *a contract can also be the owner of another one*! This opens the door to using, for example, a https://gnosis-safe.io[Gnosis Safe], an https://aragon.org[Aragon DAO], or a totally custom contract that _you_ create.

In this way, you can use _composability_ to add additional layers of access control complexity to your contracts. Instead of having a single regular Ethereum account (Externally Owned Account, or EOA) as the owner, you could use a 2-of-3 multisig run by your project leads, for example. Prominent projects in the space, such as https://makerdao.com[MakerDAO], use systems similar to this one.


[[role-based-access-control]]
== Role-Based Access Control

While the simplicity of _ownership_ can be useful for simple systems or quick prototyping, different levels of authorization are often needed. You may want for an account to have permission to ban users from a system, but not create new tokens. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Role-based_access_control[_Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)_] offers flexibility in this regard.

In essence, we will be defining multiple _roles_, each allowed to perform different sets of actions. An account may have, for example, 'moderator', 'minter' or 'admin' roles, which you will then check for instead of simply using `only_owner`. This check can be enforced through the `only_role` modifier. Separately, you will be able to define rules for how accounts can be granted a role, have it revoked, and more.

Most software uses access control systems that are role-based: some users are regular users, some may be supervisors or managers, and a few will often have administrative privileges.

[[using-access-control]]
=== Using `AccessControl`

OpenZeppelin Contracts provides https://docs.rs/openzeppelin-stylus/0.4.0/openzeppelin_stylus/access/control/struct.AccessControl.html[`AccessControl`] for implementing role-based access control. Its usage is straightforward: for each role that you want to define,
you will create a new _role identifier_ that is used to grant, revoke, and check if an account has that role.

Here's a simple example of using `AccessControl` in an xref:erc20.adoc[ERC-20 token] to define a 'minter' role, which allows accounts that have it create new tokens. Note that the example is unassuming of the way you construct your contract.

[source,rust]
----
use openzeppelin_stylus::{
    access::control::{self, AccessControl, IAccessControl},
    token::erc20::{self, Erc20, IErc20},
};

#[derive(SolidityError, Debug)]
enum Error {
    UnauthorizedAccount(control::AccessControlUnauthorizedAccount),
    BadConfirmation(control::AccessControlBadConfirmation),
    InsufficientBalance(erc20::ERC20InsufficientBalance),
    InvalidSender(erc20::ERC20InvalidSender),
    InvalidReceiver(erc20::ERC20InvalidReceiver),
    InsufficientAllowance(erc20::ERC20InsufficientAllowance),
    InvalidSpender(erc20::ERC20InvalidSpender),
    InvalidApprover(erc20::ERC20InvalidApprover),
}

impl From<control::Error> for Error {
    fn from(value: control::Error) -> Self {
        match value {
            control::Error::UnauthorizedAccount(e) => {
                Error::UnauthorizedAccount(e)
            }
            control::Error::BadConfirmation(e) => Error::BadConfirmation(e),
        }
    }
}

impl From<erc20::Error> for Error {
    fn from(value: erc20::Error) -> Self {
        match value {
            erc20::Error::InsufficientBalance(e) => {
                Error::InsufficientBalance(e)
            }
            erc20::Error::InvalidSender(e) => Error::InvalidSender(e),
            erc20::Error::InvalidReceiver(e) => Error::InvalidReceiver(e),
            erc20::Error::InsufficientAllowance(e) => {
                Error::InsufficientAllowance(e)
            }
            erc20::Error::InvalidSpender(e) => Error::InvalidSpender(e),
